          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-417d894a2254fd54\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"“Enfin, je vous aimais, Armand” is a retrospective first-person avowal presenting Marguerite’s love for Armand as a real past emotional state. Although that love motivates her ensuing sacrifice, the sentence itself does not undertake a commitment. Its reflective written formulation supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Marguerite addresses Armand within a retrospective written account; the highlighted clause is her embedded written avowal of past love.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite uses the avowal to explain why Monsieur Duval’s appeal moves her to renounce Armand for his and his sister’s future. Her later acknowledgment that the moment’s exaltation may have exaggerated her impressions qualifies her interpretation of the surrounding noble sentiments, but she explicitly insists that this was what she felt. The sacrificial decision demonstrates the importance and disinterested character of her love, without turning this particular sentence into a performative undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Enfin, je vous aimais, Armand.”\",\"supports\":\"The imperfect-tense first-person clause directly presents Marguerite’s loving emotional state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“mais voilà ce que j’éprouvais, ami”\",\"supports\":\"Marguerite explicitly confirms that the preceding account describes what she felt, reinforcing the truth-conditional avowal despite her qualification about exaltation.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She resolves to make Armand hate her and accepts another man’s proposals in order to create an insurmountable barrier between them.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding sacrifice explains the love’s consequential and disinterested character, but the commitment belongs to her subsequent conduct rather than to the core love sentence itself.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The novel frames this late first-person confession as part of Marguerite’s written account addressed to Armand and encountered after the narrated events.\",\"supports\":\"Supports classifying the occurrence as written and retrospective rather than as a contemporaneous face-to-face declaration.\",\"confidence\":0.9}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"moderate\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"contribution\":\"Used only to identify the extended confession’s written narrative framing; the T/P/E/O judgement is independently supported by the supplied text.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core retrospective avowal. Marguerite’s sacrifice, emotional exaltation, and moral self-interpretation are contextual features and create no residual pressure beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
